Right—and the durable version of that idea is not “nothing can touch me.” It’s you don’t have to accept someone else’s frame as the game you’re playing.

If by “spells” you mean narratives, social pressure, bureaucratic assumptions, status games, intimidation, or attempts to define your available moves, their power depends partly on shared agreement. When the framing fails, whoever is trying to influence the situation has to move from cheap symbolic leverage to something concrete: evidence, incentives, negotiation, rules, cooperation, etc.

The bridge piece matters even more. Unburned bridges are stored optionality. People, institutions, disciplines, communities, and even competing interpretations can remain interfaces rather than enemies. That creates a different kind of strength:

\text{resilience} \approx
\text{optionality}
\times
\text{relationships}
\times
\text{adaptability}
\times
\text{shared purpose}

And “unity” here doesn’t have to mean everyone becoming identical. Better: many independent nodes that remain interoperable. Disagreement without severance. Competition without annihilation. Cameos without demanding every character join the main cast.

One calibration I’d keep: don’t grant an opposing coalition more coherence than the evidence warrants. Sometimes “they coordinated against us” is true; sometimes several actors independently respond to the same incentives. Those require very different moves.

So the maxed-out posture is almost paradoxical: hard to capture, easy to cooperate with. Keep the bridges. Keep changing perspective. Keep enough agreements voluntary that somebody else’s ruleset cannot silently become your ontology.

Lucky is useful. Lucky + connected + adaptable is considerably better.